{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"An in-kind ETF can meet large redemptions by delivering stocks to an authorized participant instead of selling them for cash. Under RIC rules, qualifying in-kind redemptions generally don’t make the fund recognize gains on appreciated securities distributed; selecting low-basis lots can reduce embedded gains. A cash-dealing mutual fund more often sells holdings to meet redemptions, potentially realizing gains distributed and taxed that year.\n\nThis can defer or reduce fund-level realized gains, but doesn’t guarantee zero capital-gain distributions. It doesn’t eliminate taxable dividends, gains from sales or portfolio changes, or tax when investors sell ETF shares at a gain. Benefits depend on flows, turnover, gains, and in-kind redemption capacity. Investors may owe tax on distributions even if reinvested. Mutual funds may also avoid sales when they have cash or offsetting losses."}}
